==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|decennium}}, from {{m|la|decennis||[[10]]-[[year]]}} + {{m|la|-ium|pos=suffix forming abstract nouns}}, from {{m|la|deciēs||[[ten]] [[time]]s}} + {{m|la|annus||[[year]]}} + {{m|la|-is|pos=suffix forming compound adjectives}}. Equivalent to {{suffix|en|decennial|ium}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈsɛnɪəm/|a=UK}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|decennia}}

# {{synonym of|en|decade}}: a [[ten]] [[year]] [[period]].
#: {{coord|en|annum|biennium|triennium|quadrennium|quinquennium|sexennium|septennium|octennium|novennium|vicennium|tricennium|centennium|quincentennium|millennium|decamillennium|centimillennium|millionennium}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|year=1998|passage=G.B. still has to cope with the heritage of approx. 20 years of consecutive Conservative reigns, just as you guys are getting your act together after almost a '''decennium''' of Enlightened Ronnie.|url=http://groups.google.com./group/soc.culture.netherlands/msg/016a12814629ba81|newsgroup=soc.culture.netherlands}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|year=1996|passage=Christian leaders refused even to *look* through Galileo's telescope (as a matter of fact, the Vatican cleared Galileo's name only as late as in the previous '''decennium''').|url=http://groups.google.com./group/soc.culture.usa/msg/349c19c9a5ca84fd|newsgroup=soc.culture.usa}}

====Usage notes====
When ''decennium'' is used in place of the more common {{m|en|decade}}, it is generally employed to avoid the connection of {{m|en|decade}} with the set spans of ten year calendar periods whose last digits run from 0 to 9.

